    "lawCode": "Add Art 2 Title 2-F §244, Pub Health L; add §95-k, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Appropriations",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8884\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law and the state finance law, in\nrelation to enacting the Lorena Borjas transgender and gender non-binary\n(TGNB) wellness and equity fund; and making an appropriation therefor\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo provide funds to programs such as the transgender and gender non-bi-\nnary (TGNB) wellness and equity program.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection (1) classifies this act to be referred to as the Lorana Borjas\ntransgender and gender non-binary (TGNB) wellness and equity program\nact.\n\n§ 2 Title 2-F of article 2 of the public health law is to be amended by\nadding a new section 244 to include \"gender expansive\" and\" intersex\".\n\nSubdivision (2) authorizes, empowers and mandates to establish and run a\ntransgender and non-binary (TGNB) wellness and equity program. This\nprogram will be established within 60 days of the effective date of this\nsection.\n\nSubdivision (3) includes the transgender and non-binary (TGNB) wellness\nand equity program *to increase the capacity of grassroots organizations\nworking at local levels to ensure NYS invests in organizations that are\nled by gender expansive individuals. *Increase the transgender, gender\nnon-binary and intersex cultural competence by investing in cultural\ncompetency programs or curriculums that are designed by gender expansive\nindividuals.\n\n*Address inequities in funding for organizations led by gender expansive\nindividuals.\n\n*Job training, housing solutions as well as fostering leadership capaci-\nty building support for gender expansive individuals.\n\nSubdivision (4) moves to use these funds for\n\n*Community based organizations run by gender expansive individuals\n\n*Providing grants and funding for inclusive and behavioral health care\nas well as educational and vocational training.\n\n§ 3 Transgender and non-binary (TGNB) wellness and equity program\n\n§ 4 A sum of fifteen million dollars is to be appropriated from any\nmoney in the start treasury in the general fund to credit of the state\npurpose account for transgender and non-binary (TGNB) wellness and equi-\nty program.\n\n§ 5 Severability clause\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe Office of Health Equity within the New York State Department of\nHealth (DOH) aims to accomplish certain goals related to health equity\nand meeting the needs of underserved populations meaning those who have\nexperienced injustices and disadvantages resulting from their race,\nethnicity, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disa-\nbility status, age, and/or socioeconomic status among others.\n\nThis bill would establish the Transgender and Gender Non-Binary (TGNB)\nWellness and Equity Fund under the administration of DOH Office of\nHealth Equity for the purpose of funding grants, upon appropriation by\nthe Legislature, to organizations serving people that identify as trans-\ngender, gender non-binary and, or intersex. The TGNB Wellness and Equity\nFund will:\n\nThis legislature would include:\n\n(a)Gender-Expansive (transgender, gender non-binary and intersex) New\nYorkers face horrific social disparities that include unemployment,\nunderemployment, food insecurity, trauma, and unstable housing.\n\n(b)In a national survey, 18% of TGNB respondents in New York were unem-\nployed and 37% were living in poverty. 21% of respondents experienced\nsome form of housing discrimination in the past year, such as being\nevicted from their home or denied a home or apartment because they were\ntransgender, while 27% were homeless at some point in their lives.\n\n(c)In the area of healthcare, 32% of those who saw a health care provid-\ner in the past year reported having at least one negative experience\nrelated to being transgender or gender nonbinary. This included being\nrefused treatment, verbally harassed, or physically or sexually\nassaulted, or having to teach the provider about transgender people in\norder to receive appropriate care.\n\n(d)Further, 27% of TGNB New York respondents did not see a doctor for\nfear of being mistreated as a transgender person, and 28% did not see a\ndoctor because they could not afford it.\n\n(e)Many of our TGNB community members are at an increased risk of anxie-\nty, depression, suicide, low life-satisfaction, self-harm, isolation,\nPTSD, HIV, and incarceration.\n\n(f)Current New York State LGBT Health & Human Services funding does not\nprovide equitable funding to this marginalized community. State budget-\nary funds dedicated towards the LGBTQ community do not trickle down to\ngender-expansive New Yorkers in a \"fair share\" way.  Unfortunately,\nincreasing the funding to LGBTQ service providers will not ensure fund-\ning goes to the benefit of our TGNB community.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew legislation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
