    "lawCode": "Add Art 2 Title 2-F §244, Pub Health L; add §95-k, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8884A\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law and the state finance law, in\nrelation to enacting the Lorena Borjas transgender and gender non-binary\n(TGNB) wellness and equity fund\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo provide funds to programs such as the transgender and gender non-bi-\nnary (TGNB) wellness and equity program.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection (1) names this act the Lorana Borjas transgender and gender\nnon-binary (TGNB) wellness and equity program act.\n\n§ 2 amends the public health law to create the TGNB Wellness and Equity\nProgram\n\n§ 3 Amends the state finance law to create the Transgender and non-bi-\nnary (TGNB) wellness and equity program fund\n\n§ 4 Provides a severability clause Section five is the effective date.\n\nSection five is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill would establish the Transgender and Gender Non-Binary (TGNB)\nWellness and Equity Program and Fund under the administration of DOH\nAIDS Institute for the purpose of funding grants, upon appropriation by\nthe Legislature, to organizations serving people that identify as trans-\ngender, gender non-binary and, or intersex.\n\nGender-Expansive (transgender, gender non-binary and intersex) New York-\ners face horrific social disparities that include unemployment, underem-\nployment, food insecurity, trauma, and unstable housing.\n\nIn a national survey, 18% of TG NB respondents in New York were unem-\nployed and 37% were living in poverty. 21% of respondents experienced\nsome form of housing discrimination in the past year, such as being\nevicted from their home or denied a home or apartment because they were\ntransgender, while 27% were homeless at some point in their lives.\n\nIn the area of healthcare, 32% of those who saw a health care provider\nin the past year reported having at least one negative experience\nrelated to being transgender or gender nonbinary. This included being\nrefused treatment, verbally harassed, or physically or sexually\nassaulted, or having to teach the provider about transgender people in\norder to receive appropriate care.\n\nFurther, 27% of TGNB New York respondents did not see a doctor for fear\nof being mistreated as a transgender person, and 28% did not see a\ndoctor because they could not afford it.\n\n\nMany of our TGNB community members are at an increased risk of anxiety,\ndepression, suicide, low life-satisfaction, self-harm, isolation, PTSD,\nHIV, and incarceration.\n\nCurrent New York State LGBT Health & Human Services funding does not\nprovide equitable funding to this marginalized community. State budget-\nary funds dedicated towards the LGBTQ community do not trickle down to\ngender-expansive New Yorkers in a \"fair share\" way.  Unfortunately,\nincreasing the funding to LGBTQ service providers will not ensure fund-\ning goes to the benefit of our TGNB community.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew legislation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
